<h2 id="get-started">How to make money in Nashville starting this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Choose your micro-market.</strong> Downtown for tourism gigs, the suburbs for home services, Midtown for students and creative work.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick one hustle and set a target.</strong> The <a href="/tools/income-calculator/">income calculator</a> turns a monthly goal into weekly hours.</li>
  <li><strong>Get listed.</strong> Sign up for the apps, or post on Nextdoor and neighborhood Facebook groups and set up a Google Business Profile.</li>
  <li><strong>Network with hosts and planners.</strong> One property manager or event planner can send you more work than any ad.</li>
  <li><strong>Get your minimal activity license</strong> from the Davidson County Clerk once you expect more than $3,000 a year in gross receipts.</li>
  <li><strong>Save for federal taxes.</strong> Move a percentage of every payout into a separate account.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="tennessee-taxes">Tennessee taxes and Nashville rules for side income</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No state income tax:</strong> Tennessee does not tax wages or self-employment income.</li>
  <li><strong>Federal taxes still apply:</strong> federal income tax plus 15.3% self-employment tax on net profit. The <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> helps you plan quarterly payments.</li>
  <li><strong>Business tax and licenses:</strong> under $3,000 in annual gross receipts, no business license is required. From $3,000 to just under $100,000 you need a minimal activity license from the county clerk. At $100,000 or more you need a standard business license and must file an annual Tennessee business tax return.</li>
  <li><strong>Franchise and excise tax:</strong> LLCs and corporations generally owe Tennessee franchise and excise taxes, including a minimum franchise tax, even with small profits.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> Tennessee's state rate is 7%, and Davidson County adds a local rate on top. Register with the Department of Revenue if you sell taxable goods.</li>
  <li><strong>Short-term rentals:</strong> require a permit from Metro Codes. There are owner-occupied and non-owner-occupied permits, non-owner-occupied permits are limited to certain zoning districts, and permits must be renewed every 12 months. Hosts also owe occupancy taxes.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Metro Nashville enforces its short-term rental rules, and permit approvals can take time. If you co-host or clean, make sure the owner's permit number is on the listing before you take the job.</div>

